<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1" ProductVersion="3.1.4026.18">
  <POU Name="RingBuffer" Id="{9f1cac5d-a1f4-44c6-bbde-1d7bcef0e263}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[// Provides a circular buffer (ring buffer) interface with automatic overwrite capability
FUNCTION_BLOCK RingBuffer IMPLEMENTS I_AnyBufferStrategy
VAR
	anyBuffer	  : I_AnyBuffer;
	isInitialized : BOOL := FALSE;
	_overwrite	  : BOOL := TRUE; // TRUE: overwrites oldest when full
END_VAR

(*
## Detailed Description

RingBuffer is an adapter that wraps an AnyBuffer instance to provide circular buffer
(also called ring buffer) semantics with automatic overwrite capability. When the buffer
is full and a new element is added, the oldest element is automatically removed to make
space, creating a continuous circular data flow.

### Alternative Names
This data structure is commonly known as:
- **Ring Buffer**: Emphasizes the circular/wrap-around nature
- **Circular Buffer**: Describes the logical circular structure
- **Cyclic Buffer**: Another term for the same concept

### Design Pattern
This implements the **Adapter Pattern**, which:
- Wraps the AnyBuffer interface with domain-specific circular buffer semantics
- Delegates all operations to the underlying AnyBuffer instance
- Provides automatic overwrite behavior when buffer reaches capacity
- Useful for streaming data, logging, and producer-consumer patterns

### Ring Buffer Semantics
- **Push(element)**: Adds element using circular indexing (FIFO enqueue)
  - When not full: Uses WriteAtIndex for direct write at tail position
  - When full: Overwrites oldest element using WriteAtIndex (no shifting!)
- **Pop(element)**: Removes oldest element using PeekAtIndex and circular head pointer (no shifting!)
- **Automatic overwrite**: When enabled and buffer is full, oldest data is overwritten in place
- **Circular indexing**: Uses head/tail pointers to track logical queue positions
- **Direct memory access**: Uses WriteAtIndex for O(1) writes without counter overhead
- Elements maintain FIFO order (first in, first out)

### Operation Mapping
```
Push() when not full  -> anyBuffer.WriteAtIndex(tail, ...)  // Direct write (O(1), no counter overhead)
Push() when full      -> anyBuffer.WriteAtIndex(tail, ...)  // Overwrite oldest (O(1), no shift!)
Pop()                 -> anyBuffer.PeekAtIndex(head, ...)   // Read oldest (O(1), no shift!)
Clear()               -> anyBuffer.Clear()                  // Clear all + reset indices
isEmpty               -> (elementCount = 0)                 // Check element count
isFull                -> (elementCount >= capacity)         // Check element count
```

### Usage Example
```iecst
VAR
    rawBuffer : ARRAY[0..2] OF DINT;  // Small buffer for demonstration
    buffer : AnyBuffer(pBuffer := rawBuffer,
                       bufferBoundLower := 0,
                       bufferBoundUpper := 2,
                       isBufferPersistent := FALSE);
    ring : RingBuffer(anyBuffer := buffer, overwrite := TRUE);
    value : DINT;
END_VAR

// Fill buffer
value := 100; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100]
value := 200; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100, 200]
value := 300; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100, 200, 300] (full)

// Push to full buffer - oldest (100) is automatically removed
value := 400; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [200, 300, 400]
value := 500; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [300, 400, 500]

// Pop retrieves in FIFO order
ring.Pop(value);  // value = 300, Buffer: [400, 500]
```

### Circular Buffer Visualization
```
Initial state (capacity 5):
[ ][ ][ ][ ][ ]

After pushing 100, 200, 300:
[100][200][300][ ][ ]

After filling completely (100, 200, 300, 400, 500):
[100][200][300][400][500]  <- Full

After pushing 600 (overwrites 100):
[600][200][300][400][500]
     ^                ^
   oldest          newest

After pushing 700 (overwrites 200):
[600][700][300][400][500]
          ^             ^
       oldest       newest
```

### Advantages
- **No data loss when streaming**: Automatically maintains most recent data
- **Constant memory usage**: Buffer size never grows beyond capacity
- **Highly efficient**: Both Push and Pop are O(1) with circular indexing (no shifting!)
- **Simple interface**: Push/Pop semantics like FifoBuffer
- **Predictable behavior**: Always keeps newest data when overwrite is enabled
- **True circular buffer**: Implements proper ring buffer with head/tail pointers

### Use Cases
- **Logging buffers**: Keep most recent N log messages
- **Sensor data streams**: Store latest readings, discard old data
- **Audio/video buffers**: Maintain continuous data flow
- **Producer-consumer patterns**: Decouple data production and consumption rates
- **History tracking**: Keep fixed-size history of events or states

### Comparison with FifoBuffer
| Feature | FifoBuffer | RingBuffer |
|---------|-----------|------------|
| Behavior when full | Rejects new elements | Overwrites oldest |
| Push operation | Fails if full | Always succeeds |
| Data loss | Never (rejects push) | Yes (by design) |
| Use case | Queue with backpressure | Streaming data |
| Memory | Fixed size | Fixed size |

### Overwrite Behavior
- **overwrite = TRUE**: Push to full buffer removes oldest, adds newest (circular)
- **overwrite = FALSE**: Push to full buffer fails (same as FifoBuffer)

### Limitations
- No random access: Cannot peek at arbitrary indices (use AnyBuffer directly if needed)
- Overwrite is automatic: Cannot prevent data loss when buffer is full (by design)
- No notification: Caller is not informed when overwrite occurs

### Dependencies
- Requires I_AnyBuffer implementation (e.g., AnyBuffer)
- Uses Trace for error logging and verbose diagnostics
- Implements I_AnyBufferStrategy interface

### Performance Considerations
- **Push when not full**: O(1) - uses AppendRight
- **Push when full**: O(1) - uses ReplaceAtIndex with circular indexing (no shifting!)
- **Pop**: O(1) - uses PeekAtIndex with circular indexing (no shifting!)
- **Overall**: True O(1) constant-time operations for all Push and Pop operations
- Ideal for high-performance scenarios with frequent overwrites and streaming data

### Related Patterns
- **FifoBuffer**: FIFO queue without overwrite (rejects when full)
- **LifoBuffer**: Stack (Last-In-First-Out) semantics
- **PriorityBuffer**: Priority queue with ordered insertion
*)
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
    <Method Name="AdvanceHead" Id="{f89abdc7-3c28-4ef9-b3c1-e36d8b1764f7}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[METHOD PRIVATE AdvanceHead]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[// Advance tail pointer (circular)
anyBuffer.Metadata.headIndex := anyBuffer.Metadata.headIndex + 1;
IF anyBuffer.Metadata.headIndex > anyBuffer.Configuration.bufferBoundUpper THEN
	anyBuffer.Metadata.headIndex := anyBuffer.Configuration.bufferBoundLower;
END_IF]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="AdvanceTail" Id="{d4b8bf82-3b94-4456-befb-c0b089e6b546}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[METHOD PRIVATE AdvanceTail]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[// Advance tail pointer (circular)
anyBuffer.Metadata.tailIndex := anyBuffer.Metadata.tailIndex + 1;
IF anyBuffer.Metadata.tailIndex > anyBuffer.Configuration.bufferBoundUpper THEN
	anyBuffer.Metadata.tailIndex := anyBuffer.Configuration.bufferBoundLower;
END_IF]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="Clear" Id="{5660a7ee-5c25-44a4-95ab-c846847de2ce}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Removes all elements from the ring buffer and resets to empty state
METHOD Clear : BOOL

(*
## Detailed Description

Clears all elements from the underlying buffer, resetting it to an empty state.
After calling Clear, isEmpty will return TRUE and elementCount will be 0.

This operation delegates directly to the underlying anyBuffer.Clear() method.

### Return Value
- **TRUE**: Ring buffer was successfully cleared
- **FALSE**: Clear operation failed (e.g., anyBuffer is not initialized)

### Side Effects
- All elements are removed from the buffer
- Element count is reset to 0
- Buffer capacity is restored to maximum
- Logs verbose trace message on success
*)
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[Clear := FALSE;
IF anyBuffer <> 0 THEN
	Clear := anyBuffer.Clear();

	// Reset circular buffer indices
	anyBuffer.Metadata.headIndex	:= anyBuffer.Configuration.bufferBoundLower;
	anyBuffer.Metadata.tailIndex	:= anyBuffer.Configuration.bufferBoundLower;
	anyBuffer.Metadata.elementCount := 0;
END_IF

Trace.Verbose(__POUNAME(), 'executed');]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="FB_Init" Id="{3eb19cae-09c6-4812-a17b-0de3227203a3}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Initializes the ring buffer by injecting the underlying buffer dependency
METHOD FB_Init : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
	bInitRetains : BOOL;		 // TRUE: the retain variables are initialized (reset warm / reset cold)
	bInCopyCode	 : BOOL;		 // TRUE: the instance will be copied to the copy code afterward (online change)
	anyBuffer	 : I_AnyBuffer;	 // The underlying buffer implementation to wrap
	overwrite	 : BOOL := TRUE; // TRUE: overwrites oldest element when full (circular behavior)
END_VAR

(*
## Detailed Description

Initializes the RingBuffer by injecting the underlying AnyBuffer dependency. This follows
the dependency injection pattern where the buffer implementation is provided during
construction rather than being created internally.

### Initialization Sequence
1. Validates that a valid anyBuffer reference is provided (non-zero pointer)
2. Stores the buffer reference for use by Push, Pop, and other operations
3. Stores the overwriteEnabled flag to control circular buffer behavior
4. Sets isInitialized flag to TRUE to enable operations

### Parameters
- **anyBuffer**: Reference to an I_AnyBuffer implementation (e.g., AnyBuffer instance)
  - Must be a valid, initialized buffer
  - This RingBuffer will delegate all operations to this buffer
- **overwriteEnabled**: Controls behavior when buffer is full
  - **TRUE** (default): Push to full buffer removes oldest, adds newest (circular behavior)
  - **FALSE**: Push to full buffer fails and logs error (same as FifoBuffer)

### Return Value
Returns BOOL but value is not evaluated by the runtime. Always call with valid anyBuffer.

### Usage Example
```iecst
VAR
    rawBuffer : ARRAY[0..9] OF DINT;
    buffer : AnyBuffer(pBuffer := rawBuffer,
                       bufferBoundLower := 0,
                       bufferBoundUpper := 9,
                       isBufferPersistent := FALSE);
    ring : RingBuffer(anyBuffer := buffer,
                      overwriteEnabled := TRUE);  // Enable circular overwrite
END_VAR
```

### Important Notes
- Must provide a valid anyBuffer reference, otherwise Push/Pop will fail
- The underlying buffer must already be initialized before passing to FB_Init
- Setting overwriteEnabled to FALSE makes RingBuffer behave like FifoBuffer
*)
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[IF anyBuffer <> 0 THEN
	THIS^.anyBuffer	 := anyBuffer;
	THIS^._overwrite := _overwrite;
	isInitialized	 := TRUE;
END_IF]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Property Name="isEmpty" Id="{9a2d2e0b-e5ab-4c3e-9537-9bf9788b9d13}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Returns TRUE if the ring buffer contains no elements
PROPERTY isEmpty : BOOL

(*
## Detailed Description

Returns TRUE if the ring buffer is empty (contains no elements), FALSE otherwise.
This property delegates directly to the underlying anyBuffer.isEmpty property.

Use this property to check if Pop operation would succeed before attempting to
remove an element.

### Return Value
- **TRUE**: Ring buffer is empty (elementCount = 0)
- **FALSE**: Ring buffer contains one or more elements

### Usage Example
```iecst
IF NOT ring.isEmpty THEN
    ring.Pop(value);  // Safe to pop
END_IF
```
*)
]]></Declaration>
      <Get Name="Get" Id="{6b262ac3-557d-4925-8e6d-2caa16855875}">
        <Declaration><![CDATA[]]></Declaration>
        <Implementation>
          <ST><![CDATA[isEmpty := (anyBuffer.Metadata.elementCount = 0);]]></ST>
        </Implementation>
      </Get>
    </Property>
    <Property Name="isFull" Id="{44d4009b-7eb1-4ff8-a587-856072a51c84}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Returns TRUE if the ring buffer is at maximum capacity
PROPERTY isFull : BOOL

(*
## Detailed Description

Returns TRUE if the ring buffer is full (at maximum capacity), FALSE otherwise.
This property delegates directly to the underlying anyBuffer.isFull property.

Note: When overwriteEnabled is TRUE, Push will still succeed even when isFull is TRUE
by automatically removing the oldest element. When overwriteEnabled is FALSE, Push
will fail when isFull is TRUE.

### Return Value
- **TRUE**: Ring buffer is full (elementCount = capacity)
- **FALSE**: Ring buffer has space for more elements

### Usage Example
```iecst
IF ring.isFull THEN
    // With overwriteEnabled=TRUE, Push will overwrite oldest
    // With overwriteEnabled=FALSE, Push will fail
    value := 100;
    success := ring.Push(value);
END_IF
```
*)
]]></Declaration>
      <Get Name="Get" Id="{decd9c4d-b174-43d1-a249-f24603fdd711}">
        <Declaration><![CDATA[]]></Declaration>
        <Implementation>
          <ST><![CDATA[isFull := (anyBuffer.Metadata.elementCount >= anyBuffer.Configuration.capacity);]]></ST>
        </Implementation>
      </Get>
    </Property>
    <Method Name="Pop" Id="{ecc28ea5-ab7e-4704-85d5-0ad1a2481e63}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Removes and retrieves the oldest element from the ring buffer (FIFO dequeue)
{attribute 'hasanytype'}
METHOD Pop : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
	element : ANY; // Variable to receive the popped element
END_VAR

(*
## Detailed Description

Removes and retrieves the oldest element from the ring buffer following FIFO (First-In-First-Out)
semantics. The element that was pushed first (or oldest remaining after overwrites) is removed
and copied to the provided element parameter.

This operation uses anyBuffer.PeekAtIndex() to read the element at the head position, then
advances the head pointer circularly. No element shifting occurs, making this an O(1) operation.

### Operation Sequence
1. Validates that the buffer has been initialized
2. Validates that element size matches the buffer's configured element size
3. Checks that the ring buffer is not empty
4. Reads element at head position using PeekAtIndex (no removal, no shifting!)
5. Copies the element value to the output parameter
6. Advances head pointer circularly: headIndex = (headIndex + 1) MOD capacity
7. Decrements element count
8. Logs verbose trace on success or error trace on failure

### FIFO Behavior Example
```iecst
value := 100; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100]
value := 200; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100, 200]
value := 300; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100, 200, 300]

ring.Pop(value);  // Returns 100 (oldest), Buffer: [200, 300]
ring.Pop(value);  // Returns 200 (next oldest), Buffer: [300]
ring.Pop(value);  // Returns 300 (last), Buffer: []
```

### Return Value
- **TRUE**: Element was successfully popped and copied to element parameter
- **FALSE**: Pop operation failed (see error conditions)

### Error Conditions
- Returns FALSE and logs error if buffer was not initialized
- Returns FALSE and logs error if element size doesn't match buffer element size
- Returns FALSE and logs error if ring buffer is empty (no elements to pop)
- Returns FALSE and logs error if underlying PopLeft operation fails

### Parameters
- **element**: Must be a writable variable (not a literal) of the correct size
  - Must match the buffer's configured element size
  - Will receive the popped element value on success

### Side Effects
- Logically removes one element from the ring buffer (advances head pointer)
- No physical shifting of elements occurs
- Decrements element count by 1
- Advances head pointer circularly
- Logs verbose trace message on success
- Logs error trace message on failure

### Performance Considerations
- **Time complexity**: O(1) - constant time operation
- No element shifting required due to circular indexing
- Optimal performance for ring buffer operations
*)
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[Pop := FALSE;
IF NOT isInitialized THEN
	Trace.Error(__POUNAME(), 'Buffer was not initialized yet, execute method .init() at least once');
	RETURN;
END_IF

// valid sized element pushed?
IF element.diSize <> anyBuffer.Configuration.elementSize THEN
	Trace.Error(__POUNAME(), 'Passed element to pop to is of improper size');
	RETURN;
END_IF

// retrieve element at the head position if there is any left in the buffer
IF NOT isEmpty THEN
	// Read and clear element at head position (no shifting!)
	Pop := anyBuffer.PeekAtIndex(anyBuffer.Metadata.headIndex, element);
	IF Pop THEN
		anyBuffer.DeleteAtIndex(anyBuffer.Metadata.headIndex);

		// Delete index does not change counters, do it manually
		anyBuffer.Metadata.capacity		:= anyBuffer.Metadata.capacity + 1;
		anyBuffer.Metadata.elementCount := anyBuffer.Metadata.elementCount - 1;

		// Advance head circular
		AdvanceHead();
	END_IF
ELSE
	Trace.Error(__POUNAME(), 'Attempt to pop from empty buffer');
END_IF

// Pop was ok, trace message
IF Pop THEN
	Trace.Verbose(__POUNAME(), 'executed');
ELSE
	Trace.Error(__POUNAME(), 'FAILED');
END_IF]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="Push" Id="{a17a03e0-8925-492e-91b5-bc6dc26d2dd9}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Adds a new element to the ring buffer with optional automatic overwrite
{attribute 'hasanytype'}
METHOD Push : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
	element : ANY; // Element to add to the ring buffer
END_VAR

VAR
	dummyElement : AnyType; // For discarding oldest element
END_VAR

(*
## Detailed Description

Adds a new element to the ring buffer. Behavior depends on the overwriteEnabled setting:

**When overwriteEnabled = TRUE (circular buffer mode)**:
- If buffer is not full: Adds element using WriteAtIndex (direct write at tail position)
- If buffer is full: Overwrites oldest element at tail position using WriteAtIndex (no shifting!)
- Push always succeeds (except for validation errors)
- Oldest data is automatically overwritten in place when buffer is full
- Both head and tail pointers advance circularly
- No buffer counter updates (managed by RingBuffer)

**When overwriteEnabled = FALSE (bounded queue mode)**:
- If buffer is not full: Adds element using WriteAtIndex
- If buffer is full: Operation fails and logs error
- Behaves like a bounded queue (rejects new elements when full)

### Operation Sequence (overwriteEnabled = TRUE)
1. Validates that the buffer has been initialized
2. Validates that element size matches the buffer's configured element size
3. If buffer not full: Uses WriteAtIndex and advances tail pointer, increments elementCount
4. If buffer is full: Overwrites at tail position using WriteAtIndex, advances both head and tail circularly
5. No element shifting occurs, no AnyBuffer counter changes
6. Logs verbose trace on success or error trace on failure

### Operation Sequence (overwriteEnabled = FALSE)
1. Validates that the buffer has been initialized
2. Validates that element size matches the buffer's configured element size
3. Checks that buffer is not full
4. Adds new element at tail position using WriteAtIndex
5. Logs verbose trace on success or error trace on failure

### Circular Buffer Example (overwriteEnabled = TRUE)
```iecst
// Buffer capacity = 3
value := 100; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100]
value := 200; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100, 200]
value := 300; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100, 200, 300] (full)

// Push to full buffer - oldest (100) is removed automatically
value := 400; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [200, 300, 400]
value := 500; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [300, 400, 500]

// Latest 3 elements are always retained
```

### Bounded Queue Example (overwriteEnabled = FALSE)
```iecst
// Buffer capacity = 3
value := 100; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100], returns TRUE
value := 200; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100, 200], returns TRUE
value := 300; ring.Push(value);  // Buffer: [100, 200, 300], returns TRUE

// Push to full buffer fails
value := 400;
success := ring.Push(value);     // Returns FALSE, logs error, buffer unchanged
```

### Return Value
- **TRUE**: Element was successfully pushed to the ring buffer
- **FALSE**: Push operation failed (see error conditions)

### Error Conditions (both modes)
- Returns FALSE and logs error if buffer was not initialized
- Returns FALSE and logs error if element size doesn't match buffer element size
- Returns FALSE and logs error if underlying WriteAtIndex operation fails

### Error Conditions (overwriteEnabled = FALSE only)
- Returns FALSE and logs error if ring buffer is full (no available capacity)

### Parameters
- **element**: Must be a writable variable (not a literal) of the correct size
  - Must match the buffer's configured element size
  - Value will be copied into the ring buffer

### Side Effects (when not full)
- Adds one element to the ring buffer using WriteAtIndex
- Increments elementCount by 1 (managed by RingBuffer, not AnyBuffer)
- Advances tail pointer circularly
- Logs verbose trace message on success
- Logs error trace message on failure

### Side Effects (when full and overwriteEnabled = TRUE)
- Overwrites element at tail position using WriteAtIndex (no shifting!)
- Advances both head pointer and tail pointer circularly
- Element count remains at maximum capacity
- No physical element movement occurs
- No AnyBuffer counter modifications
- Logs verbose trace message on success

### Performance Considerations
- **When not full**: O(1) - uses WriteAtIndex (raw write, no counter overhead)
- **When full**: O(1) - uses WriteAtIndex with circular indexing (no shifting!)
- **Overall**: True O(1) constant-time operation for all scenarios
- **Optimal efficiency**: WriteAtIndex bypasses AnyBuffer counter management
- Ideal for high-frequency streaming and real-time data processing
*)
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[Push := FALSE;
IF NOT isInitialized THEN
	Trace.Error(__POUNAME(), 'Buffer was not initialized yet, execute method .init() at least once');
	RETURN;
END_IF

// valid sized element pushed?
IF element.diSize <> anyBuffer.Configuration.elementSize THEN
	Trace.Error(__POUNAME(), 'Pushed element is of improper size');
	RETURN;
END_IF

// Handle push based on buffer state
IF anyBuffer.Metadata.elementCount < anyBuffer.Configuration.capacity THEN
	// Buffer not yet full - use WriteAtIndex for direct write without counter changes
	Push := anyBuffer.ReplaceAtIndex(anyBuffer.Metadata.tailIndex, element);
	IF Push THEN
		// Advance tail circular
		AdvanceTail();

		// Replace index does not change counters, do it manually
		anyBuffer.Metadata.elementCount := anyBuffer.Metadata.elementCount + 1;
		anyBuffer.Metadata.capacity		:= anyBuffer.Metadata.capacity - 1;
	END_IF
ELSIF _overwrite THEN
	// Buffer is full and overwrite enabled - use WriteAtIndex for circular overwrite (no shifting!)
	Push := anyBuffer.ReplaceAtIndex(anyBuffer.Metadata.tailIndex, element);
	IF Push THEN
		// Advance tail circular
		AdvanceTail();

		// Advance head circular
		AdvanceHead();

		// elementCount stays at capacity
	END_IF
ELSE
	// Overwrite disabled - reject push when full (bounded queue behavior)
	Trace.Error(__POUNAME(), 'Attempt to push to full buffer with overwrite disabled');
	RETURN;
END_IF

// Push was ok, trace message
IF Push THEN
	Trace.Verbose(__POUNAME(), 'executed');
ELSE
	Trace.Error(__POUNAME(), 'FAILED');
END_IF]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>